# Line endings.
#
# Git normalises text to LF in the repository, and by default a Windows
# checkout converts it back to CRLF in the working tree. That is harmless for
# files only Windows reads, and quietly fatal for files a Linux container
# executes: `docker build` copies the working tree, so a checkout with
# `core.autocrlf=true` puts a `#!/bin/sh\r` shebang inside the image. Linux
# then looks for an interpreter literally named `/bin/sh\r` and the container
# dies with "no such file or directory", naming the script rather than the
# shebang and sending you looking in the wrong place entirely.
#
# This actually happened: the runtime image built on Windows would not start,
# while the same commit built fine in CI, because CI runs on Linux and never
# saw a CRLF. Pinning the endings here makes the build reproducible on either.

* text=auto

# Anything executed inside a container must keep LF whatever the host does.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf

# Configuration read by Linux services, for the same reason.
*.conf text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.ini text eol=lf

# Source and documentation. Kept at LF so a diff never shows an entire file as
# changed because someone edited it on a different operating system.
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
*.env text eol=lf
